## 2026-08-08 - US-001 Make FlateError a real exported class

- Replaced `export interface FlateError extends Error` with a real
  `export class FlateError extends Error` carrying `code:number` and
  `name = 'FlateError'`. `err()` now constructs it and keeps both its `msg`
  override and its `nt` no-throw argument; message strings from `ec` are
  unchanged.
- Added `FlateError` to the `bInflt` worker payload array, immediately
  before `err`, since `err` is stringified into the inflate worker and
  constructs the class there.
- Added `test/4-error-class.ts` (14 assertions) and wired it into
  `test/index.ts`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/index.ts`, `test/4-error-class.ts`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The story's acceptance criteria said to add the class to BOTH `bInflt`
    and `bDflt` "next to `err`". `bDflt` has no `err` -- no deflate-side
    code path calls it -- so adding `FlateError` there would ship dead
    bytes into every deflate worker for no behavior change. Added it to
    `bInflt` only. Verified by grepping every `err(` call site: none are
    inside `dflt`/`dopt`/`deflateSync`.
  - Deliberately did NOT let the class body look up its own message from
    `ec`. `ec` is in `bInflt` but not `bDflt`, and keeping the lookup in
    `err()` leaves the class body with zero free variables, so it is safe
    to drop into any worker payload later (relevant for US-002).
  - `wcln`'s `v.prototype` branch iterates `for (const t in v.prototype)`.
    Class methods are non-enumerable, so a class with methods would have
    them SILENTLY DROPPED from the worker copy. `FlateError` has no
    methods, only the constructor (which `toString()` includes), so it is
    fine -- but do not add a method to it without revisiting `wcln`.
  - `keepNames` stays false and terser mangles the toplevel binding, but
    `class X extends Error{}` survives because `wcln` emits a named class
    EXPRESSION (`;n=class n extends Error{...}`). Verified against
    `dist/node/index.min.js`: sync `instanceof FlateError` is true and the
    async worker path still delivers `.code` and the right message.
  - Async/worker errors are still plain `Error` on the receiving side --
    structured clone drops the prototype. That is exactly US-002's scope
    and is intentionally untouched here.
  - Verified: `npx tsx test/4-error-class.ts` (14/14), `npm run test:node`
    (48/48), `npm test` full suite (browser 322/322), `npm run lint`,
    `tsc --noEmit` against both `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.build.json`.

