## 2026-08-08 - US-002 Preserve FlateError identity across the worker boundary

- Added `rhyd` to `src/index.ts` and wired it into `wrkr`'s callback, so
  every error coming back from a worker is rebuilt as a real `FlateError`
  when it carries a numeric `code`. The worker-side `stack` is kept.
  Errors without a numeric `code` (worker exited nonzero, OOM, a TypeError
  from user code) pass through unchanged.
- Added `test/5-async-error.ts` (20 assertions) and wired it into
  `test/index.ts`. It covers a callback-style API (`inflate`) and a
  worker-backed streaming class (`AsyncInflate`), each against both
  `dist/node/index.js` and `dist/node/index.min.js`.
- Documented the boundary in `docs/architecture/components.md`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/index.ts`, `test/5-async-error.ts`,
  `docs/architecture/components.md`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `wrkr` (src/index.ts) is the ONLY choke point for worker errors --
    `cbify` and `astrmify` both route through it, and nothing else calls
    `wk` directly. Anything that needs to touch every async error path
    belongs there, not duplicated per API.
  - Both spawners already preserved the numeric `code`, the `message`, the
    `stack`, AND the `name`. Only the prototype was lost. So a test that
    asserts on `e.name === 'FlateError'` passes even with the bug present
    -- it is not a valid regression guard. Assert `instanceof`.
  - A NODE test cannot exercise the worker path against `src/`. tsconfig
    maps `fflate` to `./src/index.ts`, and tsx transpiles with esbuild's
    `keepNames` on, so the stringified worker payload contains
    `__name(fn, '...')` and dies with `ReferenceError: __name is not
    defined` before any real work. Import `../dist/node/index.js`
    instead, like test/3-node-min.ts does. This means the file needs a
    prior `npm run build`, same as 3-node-min.ts.
  - Rehydration is deliberately keyed on `typeof e.code === 'number'`
    rather than on `name === 'FlateError'`. `err()` is the only thing
    that sets a numeric `code`, and keying off `name` would let anything
    that happens to be called FlateError through.
  - Verified: `npx tsx test/5-async-error.ts` (20/20), full `npm test`
    (node 68/68, browser 322/322), `npm run lint`, `tsc --noEmit` against
    both `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.build.json`.

