## 2026-08-08 - US-005 Expose the raw filename bytes on UnzipFileInfo

- `zh` now also returns the raw filename bytes and bit 11 of the general
  purpose bit flag. It already read both -- the bytes to hand to
  `strFromU8`, the bit to choose latin1 vs UTF-8 -- so this is a rename
  and two extra tuple slots, no new parsing.
- `UnzipFileInfo` gains `rawName:Uint8Array` and `nameIsUTF8:boolean`,
  and BOTH filter object literals (`unzip`, `unzipSync`) were updated in
  the same commit, per the declaration-emit invariant in CLAUDE.md.
- `name` is untouched: a non-UTF-8 name still comes back latin1-decoded,
  which is lossless byte-for-byte but reads as mojibake. The doc comment
  shows the `new TextDecoder('cp866').decode(info.rawName)` fix.
- Added `test/7-unzip-rawname.ts` (14 assertions) and wired it into
  `test/index.ts`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/index.ts`,
  `test/7-unzip-rawname.ts`, `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `rawName` is deliberately a SUBARRAY VIEW onto the caller's buffer,
    not a copy. `zh` runs for every central directory entry regardless of
    whether a filter is supplied, so a copy would cost one allocation per
    entry for the large majority of callers who never look at it. The
    doc comment says so; tests that stash the value across entries must
    `.slice()` it themselves.
  - `zipSync`/`zip` set bit 11 exactly when `strToU8(fn).length !==
    fn.length`, i.e. whenever the name is non-ASCII. So fflate can NEVER
    write a non-UTF-8 filename, and an ASCII name always reports
    `nameIsUTF8: false` (correctly -- the bit is genuinely clear, and
    ASCII needs no decoding).
  - Building a legacy-code-page fixture therefore means patching. Write
    the archive with an ASCII placeholder name of exactly the target BYTE
    length, then overwrite the filename bytes in BOTH the central
    directory record (o + 46) and the local file header it points at
    (b4(o + 42) + 30). Lengths match so nothing moves, and bit 11 is
    already clear because the placeholder was ASCII. This differs from
    the US-003 fixture, which only had to patch the central directory.
  - cp866 is a single-byte code page, so `TextDecoder('cp866')` can be
    inverted over bytes 0..255 to get an encoder. Doing that in the test
    keeps the expected bytes derived from the same table the assertion
    decodes with, instead of a hand-copied hex literal. Node ships full
    ICU, so `cp866` is available without a flag.
  - Verified: `npx tsx test/7-unzip-rawname.ts` (14/14), and the same
    file fails hard with `src/index.ts` stashed. Full `npm test` (node
    106/106, browser 330/330), `npm run lint` (exit 0), `tsc --noEmit`
    against both `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.build.json`.

