## 2026-08-08 - US-006 Expose the raw filename bytes on the streaming UnzipFile

- Added `rawName:Uint8Array` and `nameIsUTF8:boolean` to the `UnzipFile`
  interface and, critically, to the `} as UnzipFile` object literal in
  `Unzip.push`. The bytes come from the LOCAL file header, which carries
  the same filename bytes and the same bit-11 flag as the central
  directory record `zh` reads for `UnzipFileInfo` (US-005).
- The name line was `strFromU8(buf.subarray(i + 30, i += 30 + fnl), !u)`.
  It is now `const rn = slc(buf, i + 30, i += 30 + fnl)` followed by
  `strFromU8(rn, !u)`. `slc` COPIES; the assignment expression still
  advances `i` in the same place, so the surrounding offsets are
  unchanged.
- Added a browser test `Unzip streaming rawName and nameIsUTF8` to
  `test/browser/zip.ts`, plus the cp866 fixture helpers it needs
  (`cp866Encode`, `latin1`, `cp866Fixture`) at the top of that file.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/browser/zip.ts`, `specs/prd.json`,
  `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `UnzipFile.rawName` is a COPY while `UnzipFileInfo.rawName` is a
    VIEW, and that asymmetry is deliberate -- both are documented in
    place. `zh` reads out of the single caller-owned archive buffer that
    stays alive for the whole call, so a view is free and safe there. In
    `Unzip.push` the source `buf` is either the caller's chunk (which
    they may reuse or mutate once `push` returns) or an internal
    concatenation that the next `push` throws away, and the `file`
    object outlives both. A view there would rot. Cost is one
    filename-sized allocation per entry, not per chunk.
  - The `} as UnzipFile` trap from CLAUDE.md is real and silent: with
    only the interface members added, `npm run build`, `tsc --noEmit`
    against BOTH tsconfigs, and `npm run lint` all pass while the
    properties are simply absent at runtime. Only the browser test
    catches it. Confirmed again this session.
  - Adding to `UnzipFile` is much easier than adding to `UnzipFileInfo`:
    there are no contextually-typed call sites to update, because
    nothing constructs an `UnzipFile` outside `Unzip.push`.
  - Chromium's `TextDecoder` supports `'cp866'` (the WHATWG label for
    IBM866), so the same decoder-inversion trick `test/7-unzip-rawname.ts`
    uses in node works unmodified in the browser suite. Those helpers are
    duplicated rather than shared: `test/7-*` imports from `'fflate'`
    (source, via the tsconfig path map) while browser suites take the
    bundle as an `f` parameter, so there is no import both sides can use.
  - `zipSync` cannot produce a non-UTF-8 filename -- it always writes
    UTF-8 and sets bit 11 for any non-ASCII name. The fixture writes an
    ASCII placeholder of exactly the same byte length (bit 11 stays
    clear) and patches the bytes into both the central and the local
    header afterwards. Streaming reads the LOCAL header, so patching
    only the central record would have produced a false pass.
  - Verified: the new test fails 7/7 of its assertions against the
    pre-change bundle and passes after. Full `npm test` (node 106/106,
    browser 346/346), `npm run lint` (exit 0), `tsc --noEmit` against
    both `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.build.json`.

