## 2026-08-08 - US-008 Stop emitting zero-length non-final chunks

- `Inflate.c` (`src/index.ts`) now suppresses the `ondata` call when the
  produced chunk is empty AND the call is not the end-of-stream one:
  `if (out.length || this.d) this.ondata(out, this.d)`. Every streaming
  decompressor funnels through this one method -- `Gunzip` and `Unzlib`
  literally call `Inflate.prototype.c.call(this, ...)`, and `Decompress`
  delegates to one of the three -- so a single guard covers all four.
- Added `test/8-stream-chunks.ts` (40 assertions) and wired it into
  `test/index.ts`.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/index.ts`, `test/8-stream-chunks.ts`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Confirmed the TODO/callback.md repro against the source before
    touching anything: `Gunzip` gave `[11/false, 0/false, 0/true]` and
    `Decompress` (which picks `Gunzip`) gave the same. `Inflate` and
    `Unzlib` were already `[11/true]` -- the bug was gzip-specific, but
    the fix belongs in the shared method, not in `Gunzip.push`.
  - Where the three callbacks come from: `Gunzip.push` calls `c(0)` to
    inflate, then checks `s.f && !s.l` for a concatenated member. For a
    single-member gzip that check is TRUE (the trailer looks like the
    start of another member until proven otherwise), so it resets state
    and RE-ENTERS `push` with an empty chunk. That recursive call does
    `c(0)` -- empty, non-final -- and then `c(final)` -- empty, final.
    The outer `else if (final)` is skipped because the concatenation
    branch was taken, which is why exactly one `final: true` still fires.
  - After the fix `Gunzip` emits `[11/false, 0/true]`, i.e. TWO
    callbacks, not the single one 0.8.2 produced. That satisfies every
    acceptance criterion (no empty non-final chunk, exactly one final)
    and is the whole of this story's scope. Collapsing back to a single
    callback would mean buffering the last member's output until the
    concatenation probe resolves, which changes the streaming memory
    profile. Downstream code must still CONCATENATE chunks rather than
    keep the last one -- that is the contract US-009 documents.
  - Suppressing messages does NOT desync the async/worker drain
    accounting. `queuedSize` is decremented by a separate `[number]`
    message that `astrm` posts on every `push`, independent of `ondata`.
    The only `ondata`-coupled worker behavior is `if (dat[1]) w.terminate()`,
    and the `final: true` emission is preserved unconditionally.
  - Test-side gotcha: helpers taking a tapzero assertion object need
    `import type { Test } from '@substrate-system/tapzero'`, otherwise the
    parameter is an implicit any and lint fails.
  - Verified: `npx tsx test/8-stream-chunks.ts` (40/40), `npm test`
    (node 146/146, browser 346/346 plain and minified), `npm run lint`
    (exit 0), `tsc --noEmit` against both `tsconfig.json` and
    `tsconfig.build.json`.

