## 2026-08-08 - US-010 Diagnose the flush(true) invalid stored block lengths bug

- Investigation only. No source or test changes. Added
  `specs/2026-08-08-flush-sync-corruption.md`.
- Root cause: `Deflate.prototype.flush` (`src/index.ts:1530-1537`) passes
  the PACKED `st.r` to `wfblk` as if it were a bit position, and omits the
  `+ 1` for the BFINAL bit that every other `wfblk` call site includes.
  `st.r` is `(pos & 7) | partialByte << 3`, so the correct call is
  `wfblk(c, (this.s.r & 7) + 1, et)`.
- Two failure modes, both confirmed by instrumenting the flush branch:
  1. partial byte non-zero -- `o = shft(st.r + 2)` runs off the end of the
     6-byte marker buffer, LEN/NLEN land at the wrong offsets or are
     dropped entirely (typed-array out-of-range writes are silent no-ops).
  2. bit offset exactly 6 with a zero partial byte -- the missing `+ 1`
     makes `shft(8) = 1` instead of `shft(9) = 2`, emitting a 5-byte
     marker where 6 are required. Offset 6 is the ONLY offset where the
     missing `+ 1` matters alone.
- Recorded first failing size for `'a'.repeat(n)`: n = 62472 (62471 passes).
  It is NOT a threshold -- 63472 passes again, 70000/100000/200000 fail.
  The real predicate is bit alignment, not length.
- Fix verified out of tree, then reverted: the one-line change above was
  applied and swept over ~2500 input sizes (0..2000 and every 617th up to
  300000), asserting both `zlib.inflateRawSync` and fflate's `inflateSync`
  recover the exact input. Zero failures. US-011 implements it for real.
- Files changed: `specs/2026-08-08-flush-sync-corruption.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - `st.r` in `DeflateState` is a PACKED pair, not a bit count: low 3 bits
    are `pos & 7`, the rest is the value of the pending partial byte. It is
    produced in `dflt` at `src/index.ts:799`. Anything consuming it must
    unpack both halves. This is the single easiest thing to get wrong in
    the deflate streaming path.
  - `wfblk(out, pos, dat)`'s `pos` is the bit position AFTER the BFINAL
    bit, not before it. `dflt` writes BFINAL itself then calls
    `wfblk(w, pos + 1, ...)`. A caller that forgets the `+ 1` is correct
    for 7 of the 8 possible bit offsets, which is exactly why this survived.
  - Out-of-range typed-array writes do not throw. A bad index in `wfblk`
    silently produces a short, plausible-looking marker rather than an
    error, so corruption shows up only at the far end in the inflater.
  - To read private `Deflate` state during a repro, instrument the source
    temporarily (`cp src/index.ts /tmp/...bak` first) rather than pushing
    an extra empty chunk to peek -- an extra `push` calls `p()` and mutates
    `s.r`/`s.i`/`s.w`, which silently changes the very state under study.
    An early attempt at this produced misleading `st.r` values.
  - Bugs in this area are bit-alignment dependent, not size dependent.
    Do not binary-search for a "threshold" and trust it; sweep many sizes.
---

## 2026-08-08 - US-011 Fix flush(true) so sync-flushed output inflates

- Applied the one-line fix from
  `specs/2026-08-08-flush-sync-corruption.md`: `Deflate.flush`'s sync
  branch now calls `wfblk(c, (this.s.r & 7) + 1, et)` instead of
  `wfblk(c, this.s.r, et)`. `st.r` is a packed pair (bit offset in bits
  0-2, pending partial byte in bits 3+), and `wfblk` wants the bit
  position just after the BFINAL bit -- the mask unpacks the offset, the
  `+ 1` counts the BFINAL bit that BFINAL being 0 lets us skip writing.
  Added a comment at the call site explaining both halves, since neither
  is inferable from the surrounding code.
- Added `test/9-flush-sync.ts` (36 assertions) and imported it from
  `test/index.ts`. It covers the eight sizes the diagnosis recorded
  (including 62471 which passed before and 62472/70000/100000/200000
  which did not), the `flush(false)` path unchanged, a four-part
  multi-flush stream, and a 0..599 sweep over pseudo-random bytes. Every
  case is asserted against BOTH fflate's `inflateSync` and node's
  `zlib.inflateRawSync`.
- Updated the spec's status header to FIXED and added an Unreleased
  CHANGELOG entry.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/9-flush-sync.ts`, `test/index.ts`,
  `CHANGELOG.md`, `specs/2026-08-08-flush-sync-corruption.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - Confirmed red before green: pre-fix the new file died at n=62472 with
    `FlateError: unexpected EOF` from fflate's own inflater. Note that a
    tapzero assertion which THROWS (rather than failing) aborts the whole
    process, so a red run shows a stack trace and no `# fail` line. Do
    not read the absence of `# fail` as a pass -- check for the `# ok`
    footer and the assertion count.
  - `npm test`'s tail only shows the browser suite. To confirm both, pipe
    the whole run through `grep -E "^# (tests|pass|fail)|not ok"`; a
    healthy run prints two `# tests` / `# pass` pairs (node then browser)
    and no `not ok` lines.
  - A typed-array write past the end is a silent no-op, not a throw. That
    is what let this bug ship: `wfblk` computed an offset beyond the
    6-byte marker buffer and simply dropped LEN/NLEN. Any time an index
    into a fixed-size `U8` is derived from a bit cursor, an off-by-one
    corrupts output without any runtime signal.
  - The sweep test in `9-flush-sync.ts` uses a deterministic xorshift
    (`seqBytes`) rather than `Math.random`, so a failure is reproducible
    from the reported size alone. Reuse that shape for the US-012/US-013
    streaming work, which the diagnosis expects to be alignment
    dependent in the same way.
  - The flush fix does NOT touch `st.i` / `st.w` / the LZ77 window, so it
    is not expected to move US-012's out-of-window distance bug. Verified
    only in the sense that the full suite is unchanged; US-012 should
    still be diagnosed from scratch.
