## 2026-08-08 - US-012 Diagnose the streaming Deflate invalid distance bug

- Investigation only, no source or test changes. Wrote
  `specs/2026-08-08-streaming-deflate-invalid-distance.md`.
- Reproduced `TODO/streaming.md` from a real `.mjs` against
  `dist/node/index.js`: `deflateSync` and callback `deflate()` are
  byte-identical and valid; streaming `Deflate` is rejected by both
  `zlib.inflateRawSync` ("invalid distance too far back") and fflate's
  `inflateSync` ("invalid distance") at levels 1, 3, 6 and 9.
- Root cause: `dflt`'s `const maxd = Math.min(32767, i)`
  (`src/index.ts:741`) assumes `i` counts real emitted bytes. In the
  streaming path `dat` is `dfltInit`'s 98304-byte scratch buffer whose
  first 32768 bytes are reserved, still-zero lookback space
  (`src/index.ts:1424-1427`), so `i` overstates the byte count by 32768
  and the match finder can pick a match inside the zero pad.
- Files changed: `specs/2026-08-08-streaming-deflate-invalid-distance.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The fastest way to localize a deflate mis-encoding here is to log
    every emitted `(i, l, d)` from the `if (d)` branch of `dflt` for the
    sync and streaming runs and diff them. Streaming indices are offset
    by exactly 32768; subtract that and the two lists line up, so the
    first differing entry is the culprit. Pairs well with a log in
    `inflt`'s `if (bt < dt)` branch, which prints the decoder's own
    `bt`/`dt` at the moment it rejects.
  - "The output diverges at byte 0" does NOT mean the encoder diverged
    early. A small input is one dynamic-Huffman block, so a single bad
    symbol anywhere rewrites the code-length tree and therefore byte 0.
    Do not take a first-differing-byte offset as a position in the input.
  - `dfltInit` (`src/index.ts:1415`) is the single construction point for
    the streaming state of `Deflate`, `Gzip` and `Zlib`. A state field
    added there covers all three; `deflateSync`/`dopt` build their own
    `{ l:1 }` state and pick up the `|| 0` default.
  - `DeflateState` and `InflateState` both have a `b` member with
    unrelated meanings if US-013 uses that name. Not a conflict, but
    confusing to read.
  - `dopt` sizes the hash table from `dat.length`, which for a streaming
    class is the whole 98304-byte scratch buffer. That yields `plvl = 18`
    (a 512 KB `head` array per stream) versus 12 for a small
    `deflateSync` call. Harmless for correctness -- all 31 shared matches
    on the EMF input were identical -- but a real memory wart if anyone
    goes looking.
  - Verified `npx tsc --noEmit` clean and `npm run lint` clean. Skipped
    the full `npm test` deliberately: the tree is unchanged apart from
    two markdown-ish files, so there is nothing new for it to cover.
---
## 2026-08-08 - US-013 Fix streaming Deflate so it never emits an out-of-window distance

- Applied the fix designed in US-012's diagnosis, verbatim: added a `b`
  member to `DeflateState` meaning "index of the first legal
  back-reference target in `dat`", changed `dflt`'s match bound at
  `src/index.ts` from `Math.min(32767, i)` to
  `Math.min(32767, i - (st.b || 0))`, seeded it to 32768 in `dfltInit`
  (or to `32768 - dict.length` when a dictionary is supplied, since
  dictionary bytes ARE resolvable), and reset it to 0 after the wrap copy
  in `Deflate.push`.
- Added `test/10-stream-window.ts` (35 assertions) and wired it into
  `test/index.ts`. It covers the 234-byte EMF prefix at levels 1/3/6/9
  crossed with one-push and 1-byte-push shapes, asserting recovery
  through BOTH `inflateSync` and node's `zlib.inflateRawSync`; a
  `ZipDeflate`-through-`Zip` case at all four levels read back with
  `unzipSync`; the four pre-fix `deflateSync` byte lengths
  (155/153/153/153); and a long-range-match assertion.
- Added the base-index invariant to `CLAUDE.md`, and flipped the spec
  header to FIXED.
- Files changed: `src/index.ts`, `test/10-stream-window.ts`,
  `test/index.ts`, `CLAUDE.md`,
  `specs/2026-08-08-streaming-deflate-invalid-distance.md`,
  `specs/prd.json`, `progress.log`.

- **Learnings for future iterations:**
  - The whole fix is one subtraction in one expression. `dflt` has
    exactly ONE place where a candidate distance becomes a committed one:
    `d = dif` inside the `while (dif <= maxd && ...)` loop. The inner
    "rarest 2-byte sequence" search only reseeds `pimod`, and the loop
    guard is re-evaluated before the next candidate, so bounding `maxd`
    is sufficient. Do not add a second check.
  - `dfltInit` is the single state-construction point for `Deflate`,
    `Gzip` and `Zlib`, and `Deflate.push` holds the ONLY wrap copy
    (`this.b.set(this.b.subarray(-32768))`). `Gzip.push` and `Zlib.push`
    delegate. So two edits covered all three classes -- confirmed by
    grepping for `s.i = 32766` and `subarray(-32768)`.
  - A too-large base is silent in the opposite direction: it costs
    compression ratio rather than corrupting bytes, and nothing in the
    suite would notice. That is why `test/10-stream-window.ts` ends with
    an 80 KB repeating-unit case asserting the output stays under 4 KB
    (it comes out at 1037 bytes). Keep that assertion.
  - Do NOT write the `deflateSync` size expectations by reading them off
    the post-fix tree -- that is tautological. They were measured on the
    pre-fix tree first (level 1 is 155, not 153; the report's 153 is the
    level-3 number) and only then hardcoded.
  - The failing state throws rather than failing an assertion: tapzero
    lets the `FlateError: invalid distance` escape from `inflateSync` and
    aborts the file. That still proves the test is a real guard, but the
    output is a stack trace, not a `not ok` line.
  - `DeflateOptions['level']` is the right annotation for a level array.
    Writing `[1, 3, 6, 9] as const` and passing an element to
    `new Deflate({ level })` does not typecheck.
  - Fuzzed the fix before running the suite: 6000 streaming-Deflate cases
    (400 inputs of 1..5000 bytes over uniform-random / 60-percent-zero /
    periodic generators, levels 1/6/9, chunk shapes
    {whole, 1, 7, 64, 200}), 126 `Gzip`/`Zlib` wrapper cases up to
    300000 bytes, and 18 dictionary cases both with and without the
    `dictionary` option -- 0 failures. The dictionary cases matter
    specifically because that is the one branch where the base is not
    32768.
  - Verified: `npx tsx test/10-stream-window.ts` 35/35,
    `test/0-valid.ts` 13/13, `test/1-size.ts` 15/15, full `npm test`
    (node 217/217, browser 346/346 across both the plain and min
    passes), `npm run lint` clean, `tsc --noEmit` clean against both
    `tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.build.json`.
